# @boundary runs
# @boundary-pending BUG-314  `.at()` returns a COPY; this pins the wrong behaviour
#
# PINS THE CURRENT, WRONG BEHAVIOUR ON PURPOSE. This probe PASSES today by asserting the
# defect, prints its ticket every run, and FAILS when BUG-314 is fixed -- which is the
# signal to rewrite it, not to re-baseline.
#
# `.at()` on a List(List(T)) returns a COPY of the inner list, so mutating the fetched
# value is silently lost by the parent:
#
#     var r = parent.at(0)     ->  var r = _zbr_at(parent.items, 0);   // COPY
#     r.add(1.0)               ->  r.append(alloc, 1.0)                 // grows the COPY
#
# THE TWO PATHS TO THE SAME THING DISAGREE, and that is the finding worth keeping:
#
#     for row in p    ->  for (p.items) |row|      const copy  -> mutation is a Zig
#                                                                  TYPE ERROR (leaked,
#                                                                  "expected '*T', found
#                                                                  '*const T'")
#     var r = p.at(0) ->  _zbr_at(p.items, 0)      MUTABLE copy -> mutation SILENTLY LOST
#
# Both copy. Only the mutability differs, so one path is visibly broken by accident while
# the other corrupts quietly. The loop is not "correct" -- it is the same defect wearing a
# type error.
#
# THE FIX IS A SEMANTICS DECISION, NOT A BUG FIX, which is why this is pinned rather than
# repaired: making `.at()` yield `&p.items[i]` would make both work as users expect AND
# make aliasing observable in a language whose containers are otherwise value-typed. That
# is Sean's call. The strictly-safe alternative -- keep the copy but make it const, so the
# silent case becomes the loop's type error -- turns corruption into a diagnostic and
# commits to nothing.

def main()
    var parent: List(List(float)) = List(List(float))()
    parent.add(List(float)())

    var r = parent.at(0)
    r.add(1.0)
    r.add(2.0)

    # The fetched copy DID receive both values...
    print(r.len)
    # ...and the parent saw none of them. `2` here is the bug being fixed.
    print(parent.at(0).len)

    # The working pattern, for contrast: fill locally, then append.
    var p2: List(List(float)) = List(List(float))()
    var row: List(float) = List(float)()
    row.add(1.0)
    row.add(2.0)
    p2.add(row)
    print(p2.at(0).len)
